.SH NAME
mvdsv \- QuakeWorld-compatible dedicated server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mvdsv
-basedir \fIDIRECTORY\fP
-game \fIDIRECTORY\fP
[-t chroot dir]
[-u user]
[-g group]
[-d]
[-cheats]
[-enablelocalcommand]
[-democache \fISIZE_IN_KB\fP]
[-progtype \fIPROGTYPENUMBER\fP]
[-minmemory]
[-heapsize \fISIZE_IN_KB\fP]
[-mem \fISIZE_IN_MB\fP]
[-ip \fIIPV4_ADDRESS\fP]
[-ip6 \fIIPV6_ADDRESS\fP]
[-noipv4]
[-noipv6]
[-port \fIUDP_PORT\fP]
[-clientport \fIUDP_PORT\fP]
.SH DESCRIPTION
This manual page documents briefly the
.B mvdsv
command.
.PP
.\" TeX users may be more comfortable with the \fB<whatever>\fP and
.\" \fI<whatever>\fP escape sequences to invode bold face and italics,
.\" respectively.
\fBmvdsv\fP (multi-view demo server) is a QuakeWorld-compatible dedicated
server.

On Debian, it currently requires a package "quake-registered" that can be generated
from copy of Quake I via game-data-packager(6).
.SH OPTIONS
mvdsv does not follow the usual GNU command line syntax, with long
options starting with two dashes. Most notably, it does not have a --help
command.
.TP
[unix specific parameters]
.TP
.B -t \fIDIRECTORY\fP
chroot to \fIDIRECTORY\fP
.TP
.B -u \fIUSER\fP
setuid to \fIUSER\fP
.TP
.B -g \fIGROUP\fP
setgid to \fIGROUP\fP
.TP
.B -d
daemonize to background (not recommended when run from systemd unit)
.TP
[common parameters]
.TP
.B -basedir \fIBASEDIR\fP
The "base directory" is the path to the filesystem directory holding the quake
game directories (id1/pak0.pak, id1/pak1.pak). The base directory is only used
during filesystem initialization. Defaults to current directory when not set.
Example:
mvdsv -basedir /usr/share/games/quake
.TP
.B -game \fIGAMEDIR\fP
The "game directory" is the first tree on the search path and directory that all
generated files (savegames, screenshots, demos, config files) will be saved to.
This directory is relative to the base directory.
.TP
.B +gamedir \fIGAMEDIR\fP
Identical to "-game". However, when both are set, "-game" takes precedence.
.TP
.B -ip \fIIPV4_ADDRESS\fP
Bind the IPv4 sockets to this address, instead of all IPv4 addresses.
When -ip is given without -ip6, IPv6 is not enabled.
.TP
.B -ip6 \fIIPV6_ADDRESS\fP
Bind the IPv6 sockets to this address, instead of all IPv6 addresses. The address
may be put in brackets. Link-local addresses need a scope, e.g. fe80::1%eth0.
When -ip6 is given without -ip, IPv4 is not enabled.
.TP
.B -noipv4
Do not open IPv4 sockets.
.TP
.B -noipv6
Do not open IPv6 sockets.
.TP
.B -port \fIUDP_PORT\fP
Listen on UDP port number \fIPORT\fP for QuakeWorld clients to connect, on IPv4
and IPv6. Defaults to 27500. The TCP port for QTV and qizmo connections is set
with the qtv_streamport variable, it is opened for the same address families.
.TP
.B -clientport \fIUDP_PORT\fP
UDP port used when mvdsv connects outbound as a client (e.g. QTV/spectating
connections). Defaults to 27001. Falls back to any available dynamic port if
the requested port is unavailable.
.TP
.B -cheats
Enable cheats on this server. Equivalent to setting "sv_cheats 1" in the config.
This makes a set of cheat commands available on the QuakeWorld client:
.br
`god` will make the player invulnerable.
.br
`noclip` allows the player to clip through walls.
.br
`fly` allows the player to fly, but not clip through walls.
.br
`give <alphanum> [amount]` will give the player items.
.br
2 = shotgun
.br
3 = super shotgun
.br
4 = nailgun
.br
5 = super nailgun
.br
6 = grenade launcher
.br
7 = rocket launcher
.br
8 = lightning gun
.br
s = shotgun ammo
.br
n = nailgun ammo
.br
r = rocket ammo
.br
h = health
.br
c = cells ammo
.TP
.B -enablelocalcommand
Allow executing arbitrary shell code from within the server with "localcommand
\fICMD\fP". VERY DANGEROUS. Only enable this if you know what you are doing.
.TP
.B -democache \fISIZE_IN_KB\fP
Set the demo cache size in KB. Will default to 16 MiB if set below that value.
.TP
.B -progtype \fPPROGTYPENUMBER\fP
Define the type of the qwprogs. This setting is usually not needed, as it is
autodetected.
.RS 11
.br
0 = pr1 (qwprogs.dat etc)
.br
1 = native (.so/.dll)
.br
2 = q3vm (.qvm)
.br
3 = q3vm (.qvm) with JIT
.RE
.TP
.B -minmemory
Set memory to 0x550000 bytes (deprecated, use -mem).
.TP
.B -heapsize \fISIZE_IN_KB\fP
Allocate fixed amount of memory in KiB (deprecated, use -mem).
.TP
.B -mem \fISIZE_IN_MB\fP
Allocate fixed amount of memory in MiB. Overrides -heapsize and -minmemory.
Defaults to 32 MiB.
.TP
.B +exec \fIMYSERVER.CFG\fP
execute all server commands configured in the file \fIMYSERVER.CFG\fP.

.SH NETWORKING
By default the server listens on all IPv4 and all IPv6 addresses, with separate
sockets for each address family. If IPv6 is not available on the host, the
server continues with IPv4 only, unless IPv6 was asked for with -ip6 or -noipv4.
If an address family that was asked for can not be opened, the server exits.
.PP
IPv6 addresses are shown as [address]:port, or without brackets when there is
no port. The variable sv_local_addr6 holds the IPv6 address of the server.
sv_serverip6 overrides it in the real IP check of clients connected over IPv6,
like sv_serverip does for IPv4, e.g. "[2001:db8::1]:27500".
.PP
The addip, removeip and vip_addip commands take IPv4 addresses in dotted
notation, where zero or missing octets match any value ("addip 192.168"),
networks in CIDR notation ("addip 192.168.1.0/24", "addip 2001:db8::/32") and
single IPv6 addresses ("addip 2001:db8::1"). IPv4 filters only match IPv4
addresses and IPv6 filters only match IPv6 addresses. IP accounts made with
acc_create match a single address, or a network when written in CIDR notation.
.PP
setmaster sends heartbeats to every IPv4 and IPv6 address a master server name
resolves to. Master server lists only contain IPv4 addresses, so broadcasts
from IPv6 servers are rejected when sv_broadcast_sender_validation_enabled is
set.

